# plugin-[[.Name]]

A ws plugin, written with [the plugin framework](https://github.com/withworkspace/plugin).

```bash
ws plugin-dev watch
```

Run in this folder, it builds the plugin, runs it in the workspace, and does both again on each save. Its app is in the web UI's sidebar, and its commands are `ws [[.Name]] …`.

| | |
|---|---|
| `main.go` | What the plugin declares: its config, its app and what the app asks it, its commands |
| `ui/` | Its app, which ws shows in a page of its own: `src/app.tsx` and `src/app.css`, on [`@withworkspace/ui-lib`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@withworkspace/ui-lib) |
| `ui/src/api.gen.ts` | What the plugin's endpoints take and answer, as the app's types. `go tool apigen` writes it from `main.go`, and it's committed |
| `ui/dist/` | The app as built. It's committed: the plugin is built with what's there, so Go alone builds it |
| `migrations/` | Its database, one `.sql` file per change (`ws plugin-dev migration <name>`) |
| `agents.md` | What agents are told of it |
| `plugin.toml` | Its name, and how it's built and started |

```bash
ws plugin-dev check
```

says what it registers, and anything wrong with it.

```bash
go test ./...
```

runs it against a stand-in for ws.

## Its app

The plugin has no HTML. ws serves the page, with its theme in it, and loads the app there. The app talks to ws with `@withworkspace/ui-lib`: where it is, its title, the theme, when what it shows is stale.

It talks to the plugin through the plugin's endpoints. Each is declared in `main.go` with the Go types it takes and answers, and the app asks with the same types:

```go
plugin.Get(p, "/api/visits", func(r *http.Request) (Visits, error) { … })
```

```ts
const seen = await plugin.get("/api/visits"); // a Visits
```

The app's types are in `ui/src/api.gen.ts`, written from the Go. After a change to an endpoint or a type it sends:

```bash
go tool apigen
```

writes it again, and the app stops compiling wherever it no longer fits. `ws plugin-dev watch` runs it on each save of the Go, and a pull request fails if the file isn't what the Go says. A type the app is sent is exported, and so is each type it's made of. [What the app asks the plugin](https://github.com/withworkspace/plugin#what-the-app-asks-the-plugin) has the rest.

`ws plugin-dev watch` builds the app whenever a file under `ui/` is saved. By hand, in `ui/`:

```bash
npm install
```

```bash
npm run build
```

type-checks it and writes `dist/app.js` and `dist/app.css`. Commit `ui/dist/` with the change that made it: a pull request fails if it isn't what `ui/src/` builds.

```bash
npm test
```

runs the app against `ui-lib`'s stand-in for ws. The app's packages need npm 11 or later, which comes with Node 24.

## Publishing it

Once it's a repo on GitHub, a tag publishes it: `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds it for macOS and Linux, on Intel and ARM, and makes a GitHub release of the builds. The tag is the version: `v0.1.0` is 0.1.0. `plugin.toml` has no version in the repo, and each build's is given the tag's.

```bash
git tag v0.1.0
```

```bash
git push origin v0.1.0
```

Then anyone installs it with `ws plugin add <owner>/<repo>`, and `ws plugin update` fetches a newer release. Before it has a release, the same command builds it from its source, which needs Go.
